1
00:00:01,800 --> 00:00:04,050
A ship can turn off its beacon.

2
00:00:04,040 --> 00:00:06,450
But it cannot stop leaving a trail.

3
00:00:07,520 --> 00:00:09,370
Since twenty twenty-two,

4
00:00:09,200 --> 00:00:14,490
a shadow fleet of ageing tankers has kept sanctioned Russian oil moving to market.

5
00:00:14,280 --> 00:00:17,930
The ships rename themselves, switch to flags of convenience,

6
00:00:17,760 --> 00:00:21,450
and swap the radio identities that tie them to their past.

7
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:23,930
The oil still reaches the market.

8
00:00:23,800 --> 00:00:26,810
So every oil trader has the same question:

9
00:00:26,640 --> 00:00:31,130
is that hidden supply rising or falling, and where is it moving?

10
00:00:32,200 --> 00:00:34,970
Ghost Fleet answers that on one screen.

11
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,810
We took the three hundred most-sanctioned shadow-fleet tankers from OpenSanctions,

12
00:00:39,640 --> 00:00:43,730
and traced a full year of their movements through Global Fishing Watch.

13
00:00:43,560 --> 00:00:46,343
Two hundred and seventy-six of them appear here,

14
00:00:46,147 --> 00:00:48,530
at their latest observed positions.

15
00:00:48,360 --> 00:00:51,090
Magenta marks the highest-risk vessels.

16
00:00:52,160 --> 00:00:56,810
The answer comes first, in plain words. Across these ships,

17
00:00:56,600 --> 00:01:00,890
the number active each month fell ten percent over the last three months,

18
00:01:00,720 --> 00:01:02,250
compared with the three before.

19
00:01:02,480 --> 00:01:05,850
The bars show exactly which months are being compared.

20
00:01:06,920 --> 00:01:09,170
Below that are the key figures,

21
00:01:09,040 --> 00:01:13,410
and where these tankers actually call. Nakhodka, on the Pacific.

22
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:18,210
Primorsk and Ust-Luga, on the Baltic. Suez and Port Said,

23
00:01:18,040 --> 00:01:21,250
on the way to Asia. We didn't draw these routes in.

24
00:01:21,160 --> 00:01:23,090
They came straight out of the data.

25
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:25,970
Now, one ship.

26
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:29,770
Suppose you remember a tanker called Longevity Seven.

27
00:01:29,600 --> 00:01:31,050
Search that old name,

28
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:33,690
and Ghost Fleet finds what it is called today.

29
00:01:34,760 --> 00:01:38,370
It's the Wolf. Same hull, same IMO number.

30
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:41,610
It began life as the Danish Torm Gertrud.

31
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:46,210
It became East One in Hong Kong, then Longevity Seven in Palau,

32
00:01:46,040 --> 00:01:48,770
then the Wolf, under the flag of Malawi,

33
00:01:48,600 --> 00:01:52,650
a country with no coastline. In September it switched again,

34
00:01:52,520 --> 00:01:57,410
to Aruba. Seven identities. Seven flags. One ship.

35
00:01:58,480 --> 00:02:02,450
Its risk score of seventy-five is not a black box.

36
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:04,970
Forty points for its sanctions listings,

37
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:06,890
thirty for its identity switches,

38
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:09,770
and five for time spent loitering at sea.

39
00:02:09,600 --> 00:02:11,770
The map shows where that happened.

40
00:02:11,640 --> 00:02:15,450
This summer it idled offshore for up to two weeks at a time,

41
00:02:15,280 --> 00:02:19,370
a pattern often seen with floating storage and ship-to-ship transfers.

42
00:02:20,440 --> 00:02:23,490
We're strict about what the data cannot tell us.

43
00:02:23,480 --> 00:02:26,290
Public tracking data has no draft readings,

44
00:02:26,120 --> 00:02:30,450
so we never guess whether a tanker is loaded. We say unknown.

45
00:02:30,680 --> 00:02:33,730
Values are ranges, with their assumptions shown.

46
00:02:33,880 --> 00:02:37,330
And every vessel links straight back to its sources.

47
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:41,530
Step back, and it isn't one ship.

48
00:02:41,760 --> 00:02:45,610
Two hundred and eighty-nine of these three hundred switched identity.

49
00:02:45,600 --> 00:02:48,570
Between them, they used eighty-one flags,

50
00:02:48,360 --> 00:02:51,610
and eleven fly the flag of a landlocked country today.

51
00:02:52,760 --> 00:02:53,650
Next,

52
00:02:53,440 --> 00:02:57,490
we'll screen all eight hundred and ninety-two listed ships every day,

53
00:02:57,320 --> 00:03:01,090
and add licensed draft data to turn this signal into barrels.

54
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:05,010
Ghost Fleet. The hidden fleet, made visible.
